  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 AM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Much colder air arrives today and persists into\nearly next week, bringing widespread sub-freezing temperatures \ninland tonight. A Freeze Warning is in effect for portions of \nthe area tonight into Saturday morning, with near- freezing \ntemperatures possible again Sunday morning.\n\nEarly morning analysis shows deepening low pressure south of \nLong Island and a 1028+ mb high building across the upper \nMidwest, while a potent upper trough digs over the eastern \nCONUS. This trough will linger over the Mid-Atlantic through the\nweekend before weakening early next week, ushering in the \ncoldest airmass of the season so far. Strong cold air advection \nwill drive breezy NW winds today and Saturday, with gusts of \n20-25 mph inland and 25-30 mph along the coast peaking on\nSaturday. Nudged high temperatures down a bit over the Eastern \nShore, as a developing deformation zone keeps considerable \nwraparound low-mid level cloud cover over the Delmarva today. \nWhile this feature should stay far enough to our NE to keep \nprecipitation out of the area, overcast skies should still \nhold highs in the 40s for much of the Eastern Shore and no \nhigher than the lower 50s over the VA Northern Neck. Clouds will\nscatter out farther south, allowing highs to reach the mid-to- \nupper 50s south of I-64 under a partly cloudy sky. Highs return\nto the 50s area- wide this weekend into early next week, with \ndry weather still prevailing.\n\nAside from lingering clouds over the Delmarva, clear skies, a \ndry airmass, and diminishing inland winds tonight will promote \nefficient radiational cooling and the first widespread freeze of\nthe fall. Lows are forecast to drop into the upper 20s to low \n30s inland, with upper 30s to low 40s near the coast. \nAccordingly, the Freeze Watch has been upgraded to a Freeze \nWarning for the I-95 corridor across central and south-central \nVA into Hertford and Northampton counties in NC. While a better-\nmixed boundary layer Saturday night should keep temperatures \nslightly higher, additional Freeze Watches may be needed for \nSunday morning in counties that do not experience a freeze \ntonight. Lingering breezes will push wind chills into the mid- \nto-upper 20s inland (mid 30s at the coast) for those early \nrisers this weekend.",
